{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"freezable-repayable-flee","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Context / Problem\n\nWe must deploy rebar where ticket data is sensitive and may **not** live in the GitHub\nrepo alongside source. rebar's ticket store is git history on an orphan `tickets` branch\nauto-pushed to `sync.remote` (`src/rebar/_config_schema.py` `SyncConfig.remote`,\n`src/rebar/_store/push.py:168` `push_tickets_branch`). We want that history in a\ncustomer-owned, SSE-KMS-encrypted S3 bucket instead of GitHub, keeping the identical\ngit/event data format and the auto-commit/auto-push flow.\n\nA live experiment (this session) proved the `awslabs/git-remote-s3` remote helper\n(Apache-2.0, actively maintained, git-bundle-per-ref + an `IfNoneMatch` per-ref lock)\nslots **below** rebar's shell-out git layer: pointing `sync.remote` at an `s3://bucket/prefix`\nremote makes init/create/claim/push/fetch work, and rebar's non-fast-forward recovery\n(`src/rebar/_store/push.py:51` `_is_non_fast_forward`) fires correctly because the helper's\nrejection surfaces as `! [remote rejected] ...`. Bundles were confirmed encrypted at rest\nwith a customer CMK.\n\nThe experiment also isolated the S3-**specific** risks this epic must own (rebar's\npre-existing best-effort-push and distributed-claim behaviors are **out of scope** — they\nreproduced identically against a GitHub-equivalent atomic-CAS remote):\n\n- **Multiple-bundles corruption**: concurrent/legacy pushes can leave two `<sha>.bundle`\n  files for one ref; `git ls-remote` then returns the ref at two SHAs and a fresh clone\n  **hard-fails** (`multiple updates for ref not allowed`) — the store becomes unclonable.\n  Upstream's `git-s3 doctor` repairs it but is **interactive-only** (crashes on EOF) and\n  **discards** a divergent head (data loss). Not usable for an autonomous agent fleet.\n- **Missing/old helper**: a client without the `IfNoneMatch` lock is what *creates* the\n  multi-bundle state; nothing currently stops such a client.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] rebar uses an `s3://bucket/prefix` value of `sync.remote` as a working ticket-store\n      remote — init/mount, create, claim, transition, and the push/fetch reconcile path all\n      succeed against it — with ticket data residing only in the SSE-KMS-encrypted bucket\n      (never in GitHub). Verified by an opt-in live integration test that drives those verbs\n      against a real ephemeral bucket and asserts a second clone reads the writes back.\n- [ ] When `sync.remote` resolves to an `s3://` URL and the `git-remote-s3` helper is absent\n      or below the pinned minimum, store init and push **fail closed** with one actionable\n      message naming the exact `pip install`, instead of silently risking multi-bundle.\n      Verified by a CLI subprocess test asserting the message and a non-zero exit.\n- [ ] The multiple-bundles state self-heals **non-interactively by merge**: after the heal,\n      `git ls-remote` returns exactly one bundle for the ref, a fresh clone succeeds, and\n      **every** commit/event from **all** divergent heads is reachable from the healed tip\n      (no head or event discarded). Verified by a real-git-repo test that forces two bundles\n      then asserts single-bundle + full-reachability.\n- [ ] An operator can enable and configure the S3 backend from the documentation alone: the\n      optional `[s3]` dependency, the minimum helper version, SSE-KMS bucket + CMK/IAM setup,\n      and the `sync.remote=s3://…` configuration are documented. Verified by the docs child's\n      acceptance checks (files present and rendering the named sections).\n\n## Child decomposition (responsibilities; non-overlapping)\n\n- **Dependency plumbing** — declare the optional `[s3]` extra (`git-remote-s3>=0.3.2`) in\n  `pyproject.toml` and add an `_optional.py` probe + a `require_s3_helper()` availability/\n  version guard. Owns: dependency declaration and the pure availability check. Delivers AC-4\n  (dependency half) and the pin AC-2 depends on. No behavior wiring.\n- **Preflight enforcement** — wire that guard into `src/rebar/_store/push.py` and\n  `src/rebar/_commands/init.py` so `s3://` remotes fail closed when the helper is missing/old.\n  Owns: the fail-closed gate. Delivers AC-2.\n- **Auto-doctor (merge-heal)** — a new non-interactive self-heal that, on detecting the\n  multi-bundle signature during push, merges all divergent heads under a repo-level repair\n  lock and collapses them to one bundle. Owns: the healing mechanism. Delivers AC-3.\n- **Docs + ADR** — document the optional deps and S3 configuration, and record the\n  architecture decision (git-remote-s3 backend + merge-based auto-doctor). Owns:\n  documentation. Delivers AC-4 (docs half) and the AC-1 operator-facing configuration.\n\n## Out of scope\n\nrebar's pre-existing, backend-agnostic concurrency behaviors — best-effort push that can\ndrop an acknowledged write under heavy contention, and duplicate `claim` across separate\ncheckouts — are explicitly out of scope: the experiment reproduced both identically against\na GitHub-equivalent atomic-CAS remote, so they are not caused by S3 and belong to separate\ntickets. Lowering the helper lock TTL is also out of scope (a bounded 60s self-heal for a\nrare crash is acceptable; the helper already auto-steals a stale lock after TTL).","id":"7a61-dcc5-d5dd-49e0","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"epic","title":"Support an encrypted S3 bucket as the rebar ticket-store remote (git-remote-s3) with S3-specific self-healing"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1786149205028312001,"uuid":"1e44e65c-30ab-40e2-9404-9d9adf3ae9f7"}